# silkify

silkify renders a picture as a physical object made of silk:

1. **Silhouette.** The subject's outline comes from the PNG's alpha channel. An opaque picture becomes a rounded rectangle.
2. **Dome.** The outline is inflated into a smooth 3D surface (a Poisson solve: a disc becomes a hemisphere, thin parts bulge less). A picture can also be wrapped on a **sphere** (the moon).
3. **Silk.** A wave simulation runs under the pointer and feeds the lighting. At rest the surface is matte (Lommel–Seeliger, the photometry of the Moon), so it looks like the original photo; where it ripples, a satin overlay and a highlight appear, so it looks like silk where it is touched.

Work through the steps in order: get the code, get the silhouette right, pick the puff, the material and the feel, render, verify.

## Get the code

The library is the `src/` folder beside this SKILL.md. Copy it into the project and serve it with the page. The page then depends on no outside service: it works offline and under a strict Content-Security-Policy ([references/troubleshooting.md](https://github.com/RussGuo/silkify/blob/v0.2.3/references/troubleshooting.md) has the line), and it changes only when someone copies a new version in.

1. **Copy `src/` into the project**, the whole folder (the files import each other), under any name. This skill calls it `silk/`:

   ```bash
   cp -R <the folder this SKILL.md is in>/src <project>/silk
   ```

   If you were given only this file, not its folder, get the folder first: `git clone --depth 1 --branch v0.2.3 https://github.com/RussGuo/silkify`.

2. **Give the project three.js**, the one peer dependency (tested with 0.152.2 and 0.186.1): `npm i three`.
   - With a bundler (Vite, webpack, Next.js) that is all; see "Bundlers" in step 4.
   - A plain HTML page names it in an import map, by a path relative to the page: `"three": "./node_modules/three/build/three.module.js"`. If `node_modules` is not deployed with the site, copy `three.module.js` next to the page instead (with `three.core.js`, which three 0.171 and later import from it) and point the map there.

3. **Import the entry point by its path:** `import { silkify } from './silk/silk.js'`.

**Only when there is nowhere to put files** (a CodePen, a single HTML file, the Lab's Download HTML), import silk.js from jsDelivr instead, pinned to a release tag, and map `three` to `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.152.2/build/three.module.js`:

```
https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/RussGuo/silkify@v0.2.3/src/silk.js
```

The page then depends on jsDelivr. Pin a release tag (`@v0.2.3`), never `@main`, which follows the repository and changes under you; where integrity matters, pin a commit (`@<40-character SHA>`; see [CHANGELOG.md](https://github.com/RussGuo/silkify/blob/v0.2.3/CHANGELOG.md) on versions). The rest of `src/` loads from the same place, and the renderer still runs in a Web Worker: a page cannot start a worker from another origin, so silk.js starts a same-origin `blob:` worker that imports the CDN one. Checked with `handle.thread === 'worker'` in Chrome 156, Firefox 157, WebKit 27.2 (Playwright), Safari 26.5 on macOS and Safari on the iOS 26.4 Simulator. Under a Content-Security-Policy, the CDN way needs the CDN and `blob:` allowed (the line is in [references/troubleshooting.md](https://github.com/RussGuo/silkify/blob/v0.2.3/references/troubleshooting.md)).

