## Step 1: Get the silhouette right

The input decides the result more than any parameter does. Check the picture before you write code.

**Best input:** a PNG (or WebP) with a transparent background, the subject whole (not cropped by the frame), long side 512–1024 px. If the user gives you one of these, skip to step 2.

**An SVG logo** works as it is: silkify rasterises it to 1024 px on its long side before inflating it (`image: 'logo.svg'`, a `data:image/svg+xml` URL, or an SVG `Blob`).

**Plain background** (studio white, paper, black velvet, a flat colour): key it out with `scripts/cutout.py`.

```bash
python3 scripts/cutout.py input.jpg out.png                        # background = median of the border
python3 scripts/cutout.py input.jpg out.png --bg "#efe6d2" --tolerance 0.14
python3 scripts/cutout.py input.jpg out.png --crop 120,40,980,1010 --keep-largest   # --keep-largest needs scipy
python3 scripts/cutout.py logo.jpg logo.png --no-flood                              # a logo with enclosed counters
```

It applies the photo's EXIF orientation, removes only background connected to the border, un-mixes the backdrop from edge pixels, crops, downsizes to ≤ 1024 px and feathers the outline. Always look at the output.

**A logo or wordmark on white:** the insides of O, B, e are enclosed by the letters, so the default keeps them opaque white. Use `--no-flood` (it keys every background-coloured pixel); cutout.py prints a note when it sees such enclosed areas.

- Background left as speckles or a rim: raise `--tolerance` in 0.02 steps, or pass `--bg` sampled from the backdrop.
- Parts of the subject eaten (pale fur, a white collar): lower `--tolerance` or `--softness`.
- Stray islands (dust, a signature): `--keep-largest`, or `--crop` them away first. Cast shadows count as subject: crop them or raise the tolerance.
- The border is not the background (a scan with a black edge, a frame): `--crop` to the paper first, because the background colour is read from the border.
- SVG needs no cut-out (pass it to silkify as it is); HEIC must be converted to JPEG or PNG first. cutout.py says so instead of failing with a traceback.

**Busy background** (a street, foliage, a room): colour keying will not work. Ask the user for a cut-out PNG or use a matting tool they already have (for example `rembg i in.jpg out.png`); do not install models without asking. Otherwise keep the rectangle (`cutout.py in.jpg out.png --rect`) and use `puff: 'cushion'`: a painting, poster or photo becomes a silk cushion, often better than a rough cut-out.

**Round things with a full texture** (the moon, a planet, a ball): `shape: 'sphere'` with a 2:1 equirectangular map; an optional elevation map (`bump`) adds relief near the terminator.

## Step 2: Pick the puff

The dome is built automatically when the picture loads. The only choice is how high it bulges:

| `puff` | Height | Use for |
|---|---|---|
| `'dome'` (1, the default) | a disc becomes a hemisphere | round objects: fruit, drops, pebbles, logos with a round body |
| `'cushion'` (0.6) | pillow | rectangles, paintings, cards, flat animals |
| `'flat'` (0.35) | a soft relief | wide wordmarks, thin or spiky silhouettes, portraits whose face must stay true |
| any number | | the height multiplier itself |

## Step 3: Pick a material and a feel

One word each. Anything you also write explicitly wins over the preset.

| `material` | Reads as, untouched |
|---|---|
| `'silk'` (default) | the photograph itself; silk only where touched |
| `'satin'` | a broad pale band of sheen across the threads |
| `'velvet'` | a dark body whose edges glow in its own colour |
| `'wet'` | a gloss spot; touched, sharp white glints |
| `'foil'` | metal: a body-coloured highlight made of streaks along the threads |
| `'paper'` | flat, warm, toothy, no gloss anywhere |
| `'ink'` | raking light, grain, the far side in hard shadow |

| `feel` | Moves like |
|---|---|
| `'silk'` (default) | medium waves that ring for a few seconds |
| `'water'` | small fast rings that run a long time |
| `'gel'` | a deep, slow dent that settles at once |
| `'jelly'` | big wobbles; holding pushes far in |

A dark picture (lacquer, a black logo) needs a brighter, broader highlight: add `sheen: 0.5, sheenPower: 14`. A painting or poster: `puff: 'cushion', floor: 0.6, sheen: 0.25`. Exact preset values and every other option are in [references/parameters.md](https://github.com/RussGuo/silkify/blob/v0.2.3/references/parameters.md). To find values by eye, open the [Lab](https://russguo.github.io/silkify/examples/lab.html), drop the picture in, and copy the options it prints.

## Step 4: Render it

The canvas sets the size: style it with CSS (for example `width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 1`). The page must be served over http(s), not opened as `file://` (the Lab's Download HTML is the exception: its picture is inside the file). A picture on another origin must send CORS headers.

On a phone, a finger that moves on the canvas would scroll the page instead of stroking the silk. `touch-action: pan-y` lets a sideways stroke ripple it while a vertical swipe still scrolls; `touch-action: none` makes the whole canvas strokable, at the price of the page not scrolling where the canvas is. Pick one; a canvas with neither can only be tapped.

```html
<canvas id="pear" style="width: 420px; aspect-ratio: 1; touch-action: pan-y"></canvas>

<script type="importmap">
  { "imports": { "three": "./node_modules/three/build/three.module.js" } }
</script>
<script type="module">
  import { silkify } from './silk/silk.js';      // src/ copied to ./silk/, three from `npm i three` (see Get the code)

  const pear = silkify(document.getElementById('pear'), { image: 'pear.png', material: 'satin' });
  await pear.ready;            // optional; pear.mode is then 'webgl', 'static' (drawn once) or 'failed' (see pear.reason)
</script>
```

The import map is required: silk.js imports `three` by name and hands the worker the URL the map resolves to.

**Bundlers (Vite, webpack, Next.js).** Copy `src/` into the project and `npm i three`, as in Get the code; never import the CDN URL from bundled code. silk.js starts its worker as `new Worker(new URL('./worker.js', import.meta.url), { type: 'module' })`, a form bundlers recognise, and the worker imports `three` itself, so both are bundled: no configuration, no `threeUrl`. `threeUrl` is only for a page without a bundler whose import map cannot be read (`worker: false` forces the main thread).

**The canvas belongs to the renderer.** In a worker the canvas is transferred and cannot be transferred again. On a remount, or when a failed worker falls back, silkify swaps in a fresh clone with the same attributes. Read the current element from `handle.canvas`, and never let a framework render that canvas: create it yourself, as below.

### React (and Next.js)

```jsx
'use client';                                           // Next.js app router; harmless elsewhere
import { useEffect, useRef } from 'react';
import { silkify } from './silk/silk.js';               // src/ copied there; `npm i three`

export default function Silk({ src, options, className, style }) {
  const box = useRef(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');    // made here, not by React: silkify may transfer or swap it
    canvas.style.cssText = 'display:block;width:100%;height:100%';
    box.current.append(canvas);
    const silk = silkify(canvas, { image: src, ...options });
    return () => { silk.dispose(); silk.canvas.remove(); };
  }, [src]);                                            // options are read once; use silk.setTheme() to change them live
  return <div ref={box} className={className} style={{ aspectRatio: '1', touchAction: 'pan-y', ...style }} aria-hidden />;
}
```

A new `src` is a remount, and so are StrictMode's mount → unmount → mount and an unmount: React only owns the `<div>`, so it never removes a node it did not make. In Next.js a server component can render `<Silk>` directly: silk.js touches the DOM only inside the effect, so no `next/dynamic` is needed. Import a static asset as a URL string (`import pear from './pear.png'`, then `src={pear.src ?? pear}`). Checked with React 19.3, three 0.186.1, Vite 8.3 (dev and build) and Next.js 16.4 with Turbopack (dev and build): every mount is `'webgl'` on `'worker'`. The projects are `examples/react` and `examples/next`; `node scripts/frameworks.mjs` runs all four.

### The handle

| Member | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `ready` | Resolves to the handle once the first frame is on screen (live or static) or the mount has failed. It never rejects; check `mode`. It waits for the canvas to have a size: a canvas in `display: none` (a closed tab, a collapsed panel) resolves once it is shown, or at `dispose()`. |
| `mode` | `'loading'`, `'webgl'`, `'static'` (drawn once), `'failed'` (nothing could be drawn: the picture did not load or decode, or nothing in it is opaque) or `'disposed'`. |
| `reason` | Why it is `'static'` or `'failed'`, in words (`null` while live). A failure also logs one `console.warn` starting with `silkify:`, the library's only console output. |
| `tier`, `thread`, `info` | The tier used (`'high'`, `'low'`, or `'static'`, also after a fallback to a still frame), `'worker'` or `'main'`, and `{ sim, steps, mesh, texture, software }`. |
| `setTheme(patch)` | Changes any material, light, ripple or motion option live (for example on a light/dark switch). `setTheme({ material: 'wet' })` swaps a preset. Scene options (`image`, `shape`, `puff`, `matte`, `corner`, `threshold`, `fit`, `angle`, `tier`, `worker`) need a new `silkify()`. Calls made before `ready` are kept and applied when the renderer starts. |
| `ripple(x, y, { depth, radius, hold })` | A programmatic touch at canvas coordinates (0–1, top-left origin): a short press, then the ripples run. Never counts as hovering; up to 4 at once; calls before `ready` are kept. |
| `stats()` | A promise of `{ frames, mode, steps }` (frames drawn, the loop's state, solver steps), for measuring sleep. Resolves at once when not live. |
| `setPhase(f)` | Sphere only: lunar phase 0–1 (0.5 full, `null` lit from the front). |
| `wake()`, `dispose()` | Restart a sleeping loop; remove listeners, observers and GPU resources (call it on unmount). After `dispose()`, `ready` and `stats()` settle with `mode: 'disposed'`. A second `silkify()` on the same canvas disposes the first. |

## Performance rules

- One live object per WebGL context, and browsers allow about 16. With more than three objects on a page, use the gallery's pattern: a WebP poster first, `silkify()` on the first `pointerenter`, `dispose()` when the card scrolls away, at most 3 live.
- The loop sleeps by itself: 60 fps while touched and for 8 s after, a 12 fps tail, then no frames at all. Only the pointer over the canvas counts: moving or holding it elsewhere on the page draws nothing. The waves step at a fixed 60 Hz, so they move at the same speed on a 120 Hz display. Phones and small screens get the low tier; reduced motion, Save-Data and no WebGL2 get a still frame (pass a `poster`).
- `breeze` and `autoplay` keep the loop awake (breeze draws 30 fps while visible). Use them on one hero object, never on a grid.

Details and measurements: [references/performance.md](https://github.com/RussGuo/silkify/blob/v0.2.3/references/performance.md).

## Verify before you hand it over

1. Serve the page (`npx serve .`) and move the pointer across every object. Each should look like its material before it is touched, and ripple only where it is touched.
2. Check `handle.mode` is `'webgl'` and `handle.thread` is `'worker'` in the console (if not, `handle.reason` says why); the console must have no errors or warnings.
3. Test a narrow viewport (375 px, low tier) and `prefers-reduced-motion` (still frame).
4. If something looks wrong, look it up in [references/troubleshooting.md](https://github.com/RussGuo/silkify/blob/v0.2.3/references/troubleshooting.md): black square, halo, crease, no ripple, `thread` stuck on `'main'`.

In this repository, `node scripts/shoot.mjs` does steps 1–2 for every gallery card and the Lab in headless Chrome and exits non-zero on any failure; re-render posters with `--posters` whenever the look changes.
